{{/*
  beads-issues/shared/rank-candidates — the ONE canonical "what do I work on
  next?" ordering rule, shared by every prompt that has to choose between
  several candidate beads.

  Before this fragment existed, six prompts each restated a slightly different
  subset of the rule (beads/work, beads-issues/work, loop-until-complete,
  loop-processing §2B/§2C, misc/continue, beads/reevaluate) — some ranked
  blocking leverage above declared priority, others the reverse, and one
  cross-referenced a ranking step that did not exist. The canonical order is:

      unblocked-only (hard filter) → declared priority (with the epic-finishing
      promotion) → epic completion → blocking leverage → readiness
      [→ bead ID asc, when Deterministic]

  Priority is authoritative and the rest are tie-breaks *within* a priority
  band. That preserves `loop-processing`'s pinned "a P0 ALWAYS runs before a
  P3" invariant while still making dependency leverage matter everywhere.

  The one bounded exception is the **epic-finishing promotion**: a bead whose
  closure also closes its epic is ranked as if one band higher (capped at
  P1). Without it the rule strands epic tails forever — the leftover
  "document XYZ" / "test ABC" children are low priority AND block nothing, so
  they lose criterion 2 to any higher-priority bead elsewhere and criterion 4
  to anything with dependents, and their epic never closes. The promotion is
  deliberately narrow (last child only, one band, ranking-only) so it drains
  tails without letting a large half-done epic outrank genuinely urgent work.

  The output is a ranked **shortlist** of the top N (default 3), not a single
  winner: deciding how many of the shortlist to actually act on belongs to the
  calling prompt (a single-target flow takes #1 and keeps the rest as fallback;
  a flow with a concurrency cap may take as many as the cap allows).

  Usage:

      {{ template "beads-issues/shared/rank-candidates" (dict
          "Set"           "the available (ready, not-in-progress) beads"
          "Deterministic" true
          "PreFiltered"   false
          "TopN"          3
      ) }}

  Parameters:
    Set           — noun phrase for the candidate set (default "the
                    candidates"): "the epic's children", "the survivors", …
    Deterministic — when true, append "bead ID ascending" as the final
                    tie-break so the ordering is reproducible across runs.
                    Automated/loop callers set this; interactive ones need
                    not.
    PreFiltered   — when true, the caller has ALREADY dropped blocked beads
                    from the set, so criterion 1 is phrased as a re-check
                    rather than a filtering instruction.
    TopN          — shortlist length (default 3). Set to 1 for callers that
                    genuinely need a single pick and have no use for
                    fallbacks.

  Self-contained: emits a numbered list plus a short closing paragraph, safe
  wherever a list is expected.
*/}}
{{- $set := "the candidates" -}}
{{- with .Set }}{{ $set = . }}{{ end -}}
{{- $topN := 3 -}}
{{- with .TopN }}{{ $topN = . }}{{ end -}}
Order {{ $set }} by these criteria, applied **in this exact order**:

1. **Dependencies first — unblocked only.** {{ if .PreFiltered }}The set is already
   unblocked; re-check for new blockers before choosing.{{ else }}Any unmet blocker is not a
   candidate at all — drop that bead, never rank it.{{ end }}
   Check `bd dep tree <id>`/`bd ready --json`; implicit schema/infra/scaffolding
   prerequisites bind without `blocks` edges.
2. **Declared priority.** Tracker priority ascending (P0, P1, P2, P3, P4) is authoritative:
   a P0 always comes before a P3. No subjective reordering; corrections require authorized
   `bd update <id> --priority <0-4>`.
   Only exception: the epic-finishing promotion. Last unfinished child closing its epic:
   rank **one band higher**, capped at P1 (P3 → P2).
   Ranking only: do **not** run `bd update --priority`. Verify last child:
   `bd list --parent <epic> --status open,in_progress,blocked`, not `bd dep tree`
   (edges ≠ membership).
3. **Epic completion.** Effective-priority ties: fewest open siblings after closure.
   No epic counts as "one remaining".
4. **Blocking leverage.** Most downstream work unblocked, directly/transitively.
5. **Readiness to start.** Next prefer immediate start without setup{{ if .Deterministic }};
   remaining ties are broken by **bead ID ascending**{{ end }}.

{{ if gt $topN 1 -}}
Produce the **top {{ $topN }}** shortlist, fewer if needed; IDs/reasons, best first.
**Do not decide here how many of them to act on**: caller picks #1/fallbacks or several.
{{- else -}}
Take the **single top-ranked** candidate, and state in one line why it beat the
runner-up.
{{- end }}
